INT. HOSPICE ROOFTOP GARDEN - NIGHT

A rectangle of raised beds and herb planters ringed by a low brick wall, high above the sleeping city. String lights glow in the warm dark. The scent of rosemary and wet soil drifts across the concrete. At the far edge, a bench faces the distant river.

DR. ANNELIESE OKORO, fifties, in a long cardigan over her scrubs, stands with a paper cup of tea and watches the city the way some people watch the sea. Her night nurse, TOMAS QUISPE-HALLORAN, thirties, drops a folded blanket on the bench and settles beside it, a battered thermos between his knees. In a wheelchair at the centre of the garden, wrapped in a quilt with his white hair standing up in tufts, ALDER FINCH, eighty-one, tilts his face to the sky, and behind him his daughter WILHELMINA, "BILLIE," forties, in a rain jacket, pushes the chair an inch closer to the herbs, then an inch back, unable to settle.

ALDER
Is that Orion?

BILLIE
That's a satellite, Dad.

ALDER
It's moving too slow.

BILLIE
It's a plane, then.

ALDER
It's Orion. You always did hate being wrong.

Billie laughs. Anneliese sips her tea. Tomas pours coffee into the thermos cap and passes it to Billie, who accepts it without looking.

ANNELIESE
It's a lovely night for it.

TOMAS
Warmest of the year.

ANNELIESE
Mr. Finch, are you comfortable?

ALDER
I'm a hundred and twelve percent comfortable. Don't fuss.

ANNELIESE
I wouldn't dream of it.

ALDER
(to Billie)
She would.

Billie smiles. A breeze stirs the string lights. Somewhere below, a siren starts and fades. Alder closes his eyes.

ALDER (CONT'D)
Your mother planted rosemary like this. Behind the garage.

BILLIE
I remember.

ALDER
She'd stand there for an hour. Wouldn't say anything.

BILLIE
I used to think she was hiding from us.

ALDER
She was.

BILLIE
Dad.

ALDER
Not from you. From the noise of it. All of us. Four kids and a dog and me.

Tomas looks at Anneliese. She shakes her head, very slightly. He stays seated.

BILLIE
Did you ever tell her you understood?

ALDER
I never did.

BILLIE
Why not?

ALDER
Because I didn't. Not until I got old enough to sit still.

He opens his eyes and looks up. A long quiet. The lights hum.

ALDER (CONT'D)
You'll tell the others I was grateful?

BILLIE
They know.

ALDER
Tell them anyway. Men say things at the end that they ought to have said at the beginning.

Billie takes his hand. He squeezes it. His other hand, the one on the arm of the chair, begins to tremble, very slightly, then goes still.

Anneliese puts down her cup. Tomas rises, and the two of them cross the garden without hurry. Anneliese kneels. She places two fingers on his wrist, and then, gently, takes them away.

ANNELIESE (CONT'D)
(softly)
Billie.

BILLIE
I know.

ANNELIESE
I'm so sorry.

BILLIE
No. Don't be. He was right about Orion.

She looks up. A thin line of stars, faint above the city glow. She wipes her cheek with the heel of her hand.

BILLIE (CONT'D)
(to Tomas)
Could you give us a minute?

He nods. Takes the blanket from the bench and lays it over the old man's knees.
